The EIP-1559 base-fee update uses only integer arithmetic with the constants \(\textit{ELASTICITY_MULTIPLIER}=2\) (so the given target of \(15\,000\,000\) is exactly half the block gas limit) and \(\textit{BASE_FEE_MAX_CHANGE_DENOMINATOR}=8\):

\[
\begin{align*}
\Delta &= \textit{parent_gas_used}-\textit{parent_gas_target},\\
\textit{base_fee}' &=
\begin{cases}
\textit{parent_base_fee}
  & \text{if }\Delta=0,\\
\textit{parent_base_fee}
  +\max\bigl(\textit{parent_base_fee}\cdot\Delta
             \mathbin{//}\textit{parent_gas_target}
             \mathbin{//}8,\,1\bigr)
  & \text{if }\Delta>0,\\
\textit{parent_base_fee}
  -\textit{parent_base_fee}\cdot(-\Delta)
             \mathbin{//}\textit{parent_gas_target}
             \mathbin{//}8
  & \text{if }\Delta<0.
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
\]

Block 1 consumes \(30\,000\,000\) gas, therefore
\[
\Delta=15\,000\,000,\qquad
\textit{base_fee}'=1\,400\,000\,000+\frac{1\,400\,000\,000}{8}=1\,575\,000\,000.
\]
This value is the base fee of block 2.  
Block 2 consumes exactly the target, so \(\Delta=0\) and the base fee is left unchanged.  
Consequently the base fee that enters block 3 is \(1\,575\,000\,000\).

Answer: 1575000000
